<NeoMyco title="Know Your Woods — NeoMyco" description="Start documenting the fungi of a familiar place, one visit and one useful observation at a time."><section class="wrap section guide"><p class="kicker">NEOMYCO FIELD NOTE / KNOW YOUR WOODS</p><h1>Get to know<br/><em>one place well.</em></h1><p class="lead">You don’t need to travel far to encounter unanswered questions. Return to a familiar place and start noticing its fungal life.</p><p>Know Your Woods is a developing NeoMyco initiative. These practices give you a way to begin now, before organized programs launch.</p><div class="guide-steps"><article><p class="kicker">01 / CHOOSE YOUR PLACE</p><h2>Somewhere you can return.</h2><p>Choose a manageable patch of woodland, a park, or your own property. Check access rules. Observing and photographing do not automatically give you permission to collect.</p></article><article><p class="kicker">02 / LOOK, THEN LOOK AGAIN</p><h2>Build a habit of noticing.</h2><p>Return across seasons and different conditions. Note the date, recent weather, route, and time spent looking. A visit without visible mushrooms is worth recording too; it does not mean fungi are absent.</p></article><article><p class="kicker">03 / MAKE A USEFUL OBSERVATION</p><h2>Keep the mushroom in context.</h2><p>Photograph it in place, then capture useful views of the cap, underside, and stem where possible. Record what it is growing on and nearby trees. Nearby trees are clues, not proof of a particular host relationship.</p></article><article><p class="kicker">04 / SHARE WHAT YOU CAN SUPPORT</p><h2>You don’t need a species name to begin.</h2><p>Post observations to iNaturalist with a date, appropriate location privacy, and habitat notes. Use an identification you can support, even if it is broad. Welcome suggestions and retain uncertainty when evidence is incomplete.</p><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/">Explore iNaturalist ↗</a></article><article><p class="kicker">05 / FOLLOW A QUESTION</p><h2>Let the records lead somewhere.</h2><p>Which fungi return to the same log? What changes with the season? Which finds need closer study? If you collect with permission, label and preserve material so it stays linked to its observation. Microscopy and sequencing can be useful next steps when a question calls for them.</p></article></div><aside class="note"><strong>A growing record, not a complete inventory.</strong><p>What you find depends on timing, weather, search effort, and identification methods. Keep those limits visible as you compare visits and share results.</p></aside><div class="return-record"><img src="https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/715327092/large.jpg" alt="Inocybe communis from Donn Bialik’s front yard" loading="lazy"/><div><p class="kicker">A PLACE TO BEGIN</p><h2>It can start at home.</h2><p>Donn’s front-yard observations connect a familiar place to a newly described species. See how the records, DNA evidence, and publication fit together.</p><a href="/neo-myco/#field-record">Open the Inocybe field record ↗</a></div></div></section></NeoMyco>
